WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Transcriber: Ivana Korom
Reviewer: Joanna Pietrulewicz

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.059
In the summer of 2014,

00:00:16.083 --> 00:00:19.684
I found myself sitting across from a man

00:00:19.708 --> 00:00:23.167
who, by every definition, was my enemy.

00:00:24.125 --> 00:00:26.018
His name was Craig Watts,

00:00:26.042 --> 00:00:28.809
and he's a chicken factory farmer.

00:00:28.833 --> 00:00:32.809
My career is devoted
to protecting farmed animals

00:00:32.833 --> 00:00:34.976
and ending factory farming.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.643
And up until this point in my life,

00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:41.351
I had spent every waking moment

00:00:41.375 --> 00:00:43.893
standing up against everything
this man stood for,

00:00:43.917 --> 00:00:47.476
and now, I was in his living room.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.184
The day I met Craig Watts

00:00:50.208 --> 00:00:52.934
he had been raising chickens for 22 years

00:00:52.958 --> 00:00:54.726
for a company called Perdue,

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.309
the fourth largest chicken company
in the entire country.

00:00:58.333 --> 00:00:59.601
And as a young man,

00:00:59.625 --> 00:01:03.434
he had yearned for this way
to stay on the land

00:01:03.458 --> 00:01:06.518
in one of the poorest
counties in the state.

00:01:06.542 --> 00:01:08.601
So when the chicken industry came to town,

00:01:08.625 --> 00:01:11.934
he thought, "This is a dream come true."

00:01:11.958 --> 00:01:14.768
He took a quarter
of a million dollar loan out,

00:01:14.792 --> 00:01:17.101
and he built these chicken houses.

00:01:17.125 --> 00:01:19.809
Perdue would give him a flock,
he'd raise them,

00:01:19.833 --> 00:01:21.268
and each flock he'd get paid,

00:01:21.292 --> 00:01:23.726
and then he'd pay off
in small increments that loan,

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.042
like a mortgage.

00:01:25.875 --> 00:01:28.958
But pretty soon, the chickens got sick.

00:01:29.833 --> 00:01:31.351
It's a factory farm, after all,

00:01:31.375 --> 00:01:33.518
there are 25,000 chickens

00:01:33.542 --> 00:01:35.768
that are stuffed wall-to-wall,

00:01:35.792 --> 00:01:39.893
living on their own feces,
breathing ammonia-laden air.

00:01:39.917 --> 00:01:42.934
And when chickens get sick,
some of them die.

00:01:42.958 --> 00:01:44.976
And you don't get paid for dead chickens,

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.476
and Craig started to struggle
to pay off his loan,

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:49.809
he realized he made a mistake,

00:01:49.833 --> 00:01:53.559
but he was all but an indentured
servant at this stage.

00:01:53.583 --> 00:01:58.309
When I met him,
he was at a breaking point.

00:01:58.333 --> 00:02:00.893
The payments seemed never-ending.

00:02:00.917 --> 00:02:02.851
As did the death,

00:02:02.875 --> 00:02:05.792
despair and illness of his chickens.

00:02:07.083 --> 00:02:14.018
Now, if we humans tried to think
of some super unjust,

00:02:14.042 --> 00:02:17.101
unfair, filthy and cruel food system,

00:02:17.125 --> 00:02:21.893
we could not have thought
of anything worse than factory farming.

00:02:21.917 --> 00:02:26.476
Eighty billion farmed animals
around the world annually

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:27.851
are raised and slaughtered.

00:02:27.875 --> 00:02:32.143
They're stuffed in cages and warehouses
never to see the light of day.

00:02:32.167 --> 00:02:36.101
And that's not just a problem
for those farmed animals.

00:02:36.125 --> 00:02:37.434
Animal agriculture,

00:02:37.458 --> 00:02:40.726
it accounts for more
greenhouse gas emissions

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:45.434
than all of the planes, trains
and automobiles put together.

00:02:45.458 --> 00:02:48.934
And one third of our arable land is used

00:02:48.958 --> 00:02:51.309
to grow feed to feed
factory-farmed animals,

00:02:51.333 --> 00:02:52.976
rather than ourselves.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:56.934
And all that land is sprayed
with immeasurable chemicals.

00:02:56.958 --> 00:03:00.184
And ecologically important habitats,

00:03:00.208 --> 00:03:02.143
like the Amazon,

00:03:02.167 --> 00:03:04.601
are cut down and are burnt,

00:03:04.625 --> 00:03:08.143
all so we can feed
and house farmed animals.

00:03:08.167 --> 00:03:11.268
By the time my three kids grow up,

00:03:11.292 --> 00:03:14.059
there's very unlikely to be polar bears,

00:03:14.083 --> 00:03:17.226
Sumatran elephants, orangutans.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:19.518
In my lifetime,

00:03:19.542 --> 00:03:24.333
the number of birds, amphibians,
reptiles and mammals has halved.

00:03:25.167 --> 00:03:27.768
And the main culprit

00:03:27.792 --> 00:03:32.184
is our global appetite
for meat, dairy and eggs.

00:03:32.208 --> 00:03:34.934
And for me, up until this point,

00:03:34.958 --> 00:03:38.292
the villain was Craig Watts.

00:03:39.292 --> 00:03:41.518
And as I sat there in his living room,

00:03:41.542 --> 00:03:45.708
my fear and my anger
turned into something else.

00:03:47.292 --> 00:03:48.542
Shame.

00:03:49.542 --> 00:03:51.851
My whole life I had spent blaming him,

00:03:51.875 --> 00:03:53.143
hating him,

00:03:53.167 --> 00:03:54.542
I even wished him ill.

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:58.518
I had never once

00:03:58.542 --> 00:04:02.125
thought about his struggle, his choices.

00:04:02.958 --> 00:04:05.434
Could he be a potential ally?

00:04:05.458 --> 00:04:07.434
I never had thought

00:04:07.458 --> 00:04:09.958
he feels as trapped as the chickens.

00:04:12.042 --> 00:04:14.726
So we had been sitting there for hours

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:17.976
and the midday turned into afternoon,

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.893
turned into dusk, turned into darkness,

00:04:19.917 --> 00:04:21.184
and he suddenly said,

00:04:21.208 --> 00:04:24.059
"OK, are you ready to see the chickens?"

00:04:24.083 --> 00:04:26.434
So under the cover of darkness,

00:04:26.458 --> 00:04:29.768
we walked towards one
of these long, gray houses.

00:04:29.792 --> 00:04:32.393
And he swung open the door

00:04:32.417 --> 00:04:33.684
and we stepped inside,

00:04:33.708 --> 00:04:37.059
and we were hit
with this overpowering smell

00:04:37.083 --> 00:04:39.684
and every muscle in my body tensed up

00:04:39.708 --> 00:04:42.059
and I coughed and my eyes teared.

00:04:42.083 --> 00:04:45.518
I was too overwhelmed
by my own physical discomfort,

00:04:45.542 --> 00:04:47.268
I didn't even look around at first,

00:04:47.292 --> 00:04:48.559
but when I did,

00:04:48.583 --> 00:04:51.643
what I saw brought me to tears.

00:04:51.667 --> 00:04:55.143
Tens of thousands of newly hatched chicks

00:04:55.167 --> 00:04:58.226
in this darkened warehouse

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:01.000
with nowhere to go and nothing to do.

00:05:01.917 --> 00:05:05.476
Over the next few months,
I returned many times,

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:07.226
with filmmaker Raegan Hodge,

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:09.809
to record, to understand,

00:05:09.833 --> 00:05:11.559
to build trust with Craig.

00:05:11.583 --> 00:05:13.351
And I walked his houses with him

00:05:13.375 --> 00:05:15.934
as he picked up dead and dying birds,

00:05:15.958 --> 00:05:18.976
birds with messed-up legs
and trouble breathing

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.851
and difficulty walking.

00:05:20.875 --> 00:05:23.625
And all of this we caught on film.

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:27.101
And then we decided to do something

00:05:27.125 --> 00:05:30.309
I don't think either he or I
ever expected to do when we first met.

00:05:30.333 --> 00:05:32.393
We decided to release that footage.

00:05:32.417 --> 00:05:34.809
And that was really risky for both of us.

00:05:34.833 --> 00:05:37.851
It was risky for him
because he could lose his income,

00:05:37.875 --> 00:05:42.101
his home, his land,
his neighbors hating him.

00:05:42.125 --> 00:05:45.309
And I could risk
getting my organization sued,

00:05:45.333 --> 00:05:47.851
or being the reason
that he would lose everything,

00:05:47.875 --> 00:05:51.125
but we had to do it anyway.

00:05:53.583 --> 00:05:56.143
"The New York Times" broke the story

00:05:56.167 --> 00:05:58.518
and within 24 hours,

00:05:58.542 --> 00:06:02.184
a million people had seen our video.

00:06:02.208 --> 00:06:04.893
It went viral by every definition,

00:06:04.917 --> 00:06:08.059
and suddenly we had this global platform

00:06:08.083 --> 00:06:11.125
for talking about factory farming.

00:06:12.792 --> 00:06:15.500
And working with Craig got me thinking.

00:06:16.458 --> 00:06:19.833
What other unlikely allies are out there?

00:06:21.333 --> 00:06:22.601
What other progress,

00:06:22.625 --> 00:06:27.000
what other lessons can I learn
if I cross those enemy lines?

00:06:28.667 --> 00:06:31.143
The first lesson I learned

00:06:31.167 --> 00:06:35.250
is that we have to become comfortable
with being uncomfortable.

00:06:36.333 --> 00:06:38.768
Only talking to people who agree with us,

00:06:38.792 --> 00:06:41.458
it's not going to get us to the solution.

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:46.851
We have to be willing
to enter other people's space.

00:06:46.875 --> 00:06:48.143
Because quite often,

00:06:48.167 --> 00:06:52.351
the enemy has the power
to change the problem

00:06:52.375 --> 00:06:53.726
that we're trying to solve.

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:57.143
In my case, I'm not in charge
of a single chicken.

00:06:57.167 --> 00:06:59.268
The farmer is and so are
the meat companies.

00:06:59.292 --> 00:07:02.500
So I need to enter their space
if I want to solve the problem.

00:07:03.333 --> 00:07:06.309
And a couple of years
after working with Craig,

00:07:06.333 --> 00:07:08.851
I did something again
I never expected to do.

00:07:08.875 --> 00:07:12.143
I sat down with an even bigger
so-called enemy:

00:07:12.167 --> 00:07:14.643
Jim Perdue himself.

00:07:14.667 --> 00:07:17.917
The man I had made the villain
of my viral video.

00:07:19.667 --> 00:07:22.059
And again, through difficult conversations

00:07:22.083 --> 00:07:25.184
and being uncomfortable,

00:07:25.208 --> 00:07:27.934
Perdue came out with the first
animal care policy

00:07:27.958 --> 00:07:30.333
of any poultry company.

00:07:31.042 --> 00:07:33.351
In it, they agreed to do

00:07:33.375 --> 00:07:36.934
some of the things we had criticized them
for not doing in the viral video,

00:07:36.958 --> 00:07:39.643
like put windows into houses.

00:07:39.667 --> 00:07:41.042
And pay for them.

00:07:42.917 --> 00:07:45.417
And that was a really
important lesson for me.

00:07:46.625 --> 00:07:47.976
The second lesson

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.684
is that when we sit down to negotiate

00:07:52.708 --> 00:07:54.434
with the enemy,

00:07:54.458 --> 00:07:57.768
we need to remember,
there's a human being in front of us

00:07:57.792 --> 00:08:01.268
that very likely
has more in common with us

00:08:01.292 --> 00:08:03.601
than we care to admit.

00:08:03.625 --> 00:08:05.018
And I learned this firsthand

00:08:05.042 --> 00:08:09.393
when I was invited to visit
at a major poultry company's headquarters.

00:08:09.417 --> 00:08:13.434
And it was the first time
that my organization had been invited,

00:08:13.458 --> 00:08:17.684
and any organization had been invited,
to visit with them.

00:08:17.708 --> 00:08:20.059
And as we walked through the corridor,

00:08:20.083 --> 00:08:23.184
there were literally people
who were peeking our from the cubicles

00:08:23.208 --> 00:08:26.476
to get a quick look at what does
an animal rights activist look like,

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:27.809
and we walked --

00:08:27.833 --> 00:08:30.643
I look like this, so I don't know
what they were expecting.

00:08:30.667 --> 00:08:31.684
(Laughter)

00:08:31.708 --> 00:08:35.184
But as we walked into the boardroom,

00:08:35.208 --> 00:08:38.393
there was an executive
who was in charge, sitting there.

00:08:38.417 --> 00:08:40.018
And his arms were crossed

00:08:40.042 --> 00:08:42.143
and he did not want me to be there.

00:08:42.167 --> 00:08:44.726
And I flipped open my laptop,

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:46.518
and my background photo came up,

00:08:46.542 --> 00:08:49.184
and it was a picture of my three kids.

00:08:49.208 --> 00:08:52.476
My daughter clearly looks
different than my sons.

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:56.309
And when he saw that photo
he uncrossed his arms

00:08:56.333 --> 00:08:59.684
and he tilted his head
and he leaned forward and he said,

00:08:59.708 --> 00:09:01.268
"Are those your kids?"

00:09:01.292 --> 00:09:02.934
And I said, "Yeah.

00:09:02.958 --> 00:09:05.164
I just got back 
from adopting my daughter -- "

00:09:05.188 --> 00:09:07.976
And I babbled on way too much
for a professional meeting.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:11.934
And he stopped me and he said,

00:09:11.958 --> 00:09:14.000
"I have two adopted kids."

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:18.684
And for the next 20 minutes,

00:09:18.708 --> 00:09:20.226
we just talked about that.

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:22.684
We talked about adoption
and being a parent

00:09:22.708 --> 00:09:25.643
and in those moments,

00:09:25.667 --> 00:09:28.101
we forgot who we were supposed to be

00:09:28.125 --> 00:09:29.458
at that table.

00:09:30.083 --> 00:09:32.101
And the walls came down,

00:09:32.125 --> 00:09:35.768
and a bridge was built
and we crossed this divide.

00:09:35.792 --> 00:09:39.018
And more progress
was made with that company

00:09:39.042 --> 00:09:42.625
because of that human
connection that we made.

00:09:44.167 --> 00:09:46.393
My last lesson for you

00:09:46.417 --> 00:09:50.684
is that when we sit down
with the so-called enemy,

00:09:50.708 --> 00:09:53.351
we need to look for the win-win.

00:09:53.375 --> 00:09:56.101
Instead of going in
with farmers like Craig Watts

00:09:56.125 --> 00:09:58.476
and thinking, "I need
to put them out of farming,"

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:02.018
I started to think how can I help them
be different kinds of farmers,

00:10:02.042 --> 00:10:05.476
like, growing hemp or mushrooms.

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:09.333
And a farmer I later worked with
did exactly that.

00:10:10.167 --> 00:10:13.184
He did do the exposé with me and filmed,

00:10:13.208 --> 00:10:15.268
and we went with
"The New York Times" again,

00:10:15.292 --> 00:10:16.559
but he went beyond that.

00:10:16.583 --> 00:10:18.226
He quit chicken factory farming,

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:20.143
and it turns out

00:10:20.167 --> 00:10:22.768
that those big, long, gray warehouses

00:10:22.792 --> 00:10:25.476
are the perfect environment

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:27.351
for growing something else.

00:10:27.375 --> 00:10:31.309
(Laughter)

00:10:31.333 --> 00:10:35.393
(Applause)

00:10:35.417 --> 00:10:37.643
That's hemp, people, that's hemp.

00:10:37.667 --> 00:10:40.375
(Laughter)

00:10:42.042 --> 00:10:47.309
Here is an environmentally friendly way
to stay on the land,

00:10:47.333 --> 00:10:49.143
to pay the bills,

00:10:49.167 --> 00:10:51.643
that a vegan animal rights activist

00:10:51.667 --> 00:10:53.809
and a chicken farmer can get behind.

00:10:53.833 --> 00:10:57.143
(Laughter)

00:10:57.167 --> 00:10:59.226
And instead of thinking,

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:03.143
how can I get these big
meat companies out of business,

00:11:03.167 --> 00:11:07.226
I started thinking, how can I help them
evolve into a different kind of business.

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:10.226
One where the protein doesn't come
from slaughtered animals,

00:11:10.250 --> 00:11:12.518
but rather, plants.

00:11:12.542 --> 00:11:13.809
And believe it or not,

00:11:13.833 --> 00:11:17.976
these big companies are starting
to move their ships in that direction.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:23.768
Cargill and Tyson and Perdue
are adding plant-based proteins

00:11:23.792 --> 00:11:26.018
into their supply chain.

00:11:26.042 --> 00:11:29.184
And Perdue himself said that,

00:11:29.208 --> 00:11:31.351
"Our company is a premium protein company,

00:11:31.375 --> 00:11:35.393
and nothing about that says
that it has to come from animals."

00:11:35.417 --> 00:11:39.476
And in my own home town of Atlanta,

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:42.809
KFC did a one-day trial with Beyond Meat,

00:11:42.833 --> 00:11:45.226
for plant-based chicken nuggets.

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:46.559
And it was insane,

00:11:46.583 --> 00:11:48.851
there were lines
wrapped around the corner,

00:11:48.875 --> 00:11:50.976
there was traffic stopped
in all directions,

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:53.893
you would think they were giving out
free Beyoncé tickets.

00:11:53.917 --> 00:11:57.833
People are ready for this shift.

00:11:59.083 --> 00:12:01.934
We need to build a big tent

00:12:01.958 --> 00:12:03.893
that everyone can get under.

00:12:03.917 --> 00:12:06.476
From the chicken factory farmer,

00:12:06.500 --> 00:12:08.059
to the mega meat company,

00:12:08.083 --> 00:12:10.684
to the animal rights activist.

00:12:10.708 --> 00:12:12.726
And these lessons,

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:14.518
they can apply to many causes,

00:12:14.542 --> 00:12:18.809
whether it be with a problem with an ex,

00:12:18.833 --> 00:12:21.125
a neighbor or an in-law.

00:12:22.042 --> 00:12:27.226
Or with some of the biggest problems
of exploitation and oppression,

00:12:27.250 --> 00:12:29.601
like factory farming,

00:12:29.625 --> 00:12:33.792
or misogyny or racism or climate change.

00:12:35.042 --> 00:12:39.059
The world's smallest and biggest problems,

00:12:39.083 --> 00:12:41.768
they won't be solved
by beating down our enemies

00:12:41.792 --> 00:12:45.583
but by finding these
win-win pathways together.

00:12:46.625 --> 00:12:49.268
It does require us

00:12:49.292 --> 00:12:53.792
to let go of that idea of us versus them

00:12:54.667 --> 00:12:58.018
and realize there's only one us,

00:12:58.042 --> 00:12:59.601
all of us,

00:12:59.625 --> 00:13:01.708
against an unjust system.

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:04.643
And it is difficult,

00:13:04.667 --> 00:13:06.833
and messy, and uncomfortable.

00:13:07.917 --> 00:13:10.226
But it is critical.

00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:13.726
And maybe the only way

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:16.393
to build that compassionate food system

00:13:16.417 --> 00:13:20.309
that we all, from the chicken
to the chicken farmer

00:13:20.333 --> 00:13:23.726
to the mega meat company, to all of us,

00:13:23.750 --> 00:13:25.000
deserve.

00:13:26.208 --> 00:13:27.476
Thank you.

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:32.708
(Applause)

